_margin": 2.44, "analyst_adjustment": -0.44, "key_factors": ["Open seat after Chuck Edwards withdrew, removing incumbent advantage", "Jamie Ager's strong fundraising ($3.0M raised, $2.2M cash) with no outside opposition spending", "Kalshi and Polymarket price the Democrat near 76-78%, though PredictIt leans Republican", "District's R+5 partisan lean and a single, Democratic-sponsored September poll showing only a modest Ager edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican-leaning district, but the open seat, Ager's large financial edge, DCCC ad investment, and markets pricing the Democrat in the mid-to-high 70s justify a modest Democratic tilt. Expert raters mostly call it a tossup, so the margin stays narrow.", "watch": "Any late polling or a shift in prediction-market pricing toward Balkcom, especially on Helene recovery or cost-of-living messaging, could flip this back to a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "NC-11 is an open seat this cycle after Republican incumbent Chuck Edwards withdrew from the ballot, leaving Republican Jennifer Balkcom to face Democrat Jamie Ager in a western North Carolina district with a Republican lean of about five points. Ager has raised over $3 million and holds a large cash advantage, and the DCCC has begun airing ads in the race, while no outside groups are spending against him.\n\nMost expert raters call the race a tossup, though Split Ticket rates it a Democratic flip and Inside Elections leans Republican. The lone recent poll, from a Democratic-leaning pollster, shows Ager narrowly ahead. Prediction markets diverge: Kalshi and Polymarket favor the Democrat strongly, while PredictIt leans Republican. The race sits on a Republican-tilting baseline but with genuine Democratic opportunity.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem"